00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:57.962
Let's go back a little bit.

00:02:57.962 --> 00:02:58.920
Where did we come from?

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.211
So about four years ago,
pretty much exactly four years

00:03:01.211 --> 00:03:04.350
ago, Dropcam was bought by Nest.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.570
And Dropcam really has
been the foundation of what

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.910
is known as Nest Cam today.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:12.690
And then six months prior
to that, Google bought Nest.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:16.110
So that brings up
the obvious question,

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:18.390
did they make you do this?

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:20.100
Did they make you move
onto Google Cloud?

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.320
And I want to debunk that
because it would actually

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:25.440
cut the talk in half because
we talk about our decision

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:26.190
process.

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:28.680
So the short answer is no.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.020
Up until recently, we're
an Alphabet company.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:32.940
And we call them "other bets."

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:35.980
And as a bet, you really
make your own decisions.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:38.640
And it's also fair to say
there's good parts of Nest that

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:40.080
continue to run on AWS.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.640
And there's other
bets that are not

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.450
on GCP that are also either
self-hosted or in other cloud

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:46.080
providers.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:49.230
So the decision process
that we're talking through

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:50.945
is completely self-made.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:54.520
So where were we?

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:56.950
About four years ago, Nest was
already a pretty big place.

00:03:56.950 --> 00:03:58.449
We had thermostats,
smoke detectors.

00:03:58.449 --> 00:04:00.250
We had cameras.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:03.670
Since then, we've added more
cameras, more thermostats,

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:07.540
security system, et
cetera, et cetera.

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:09.850
Today, I really want
to focus on the camera

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:12.430
side of the business.

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:16.730
And the camera in
AWS was built--

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:20.829
or the camera service was
built with AWS components

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:24.760
such as EC2, S3, DynamoDB, and
then for analytics, Redshift

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:26.920
and Kinesis.

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:31.200
I like to use the analogy
of a box of LEGOs.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.810
Each cloud provider has
their own kind of tiles.

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:37.340
And some of them
are very compatible.

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:40.420
So if you compare S3 to
Google Cloud Storage,

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:41.800
they fit very well together.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:44.980
But then there's other
services, such as DynamoDB

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:49.330
that you have something like
that in the Google Cloud box,

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:53.140
but you actually need to
adjust your build quite a bit

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:55.750
to create the same
customer experience.

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:58.360
So that's what I'm going
to walk you through,

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:03.950
how we found replacement
technologies for all of these.

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:06.400
The other thing I wanted
to say real quick, as we

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:08.860
were acquired, much
smaller operations.

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:10.450
We were operating
on tens of gigabits

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:13.330
of recording, which was
the equivalent of hundreds

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:14.380
of years per day.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:17.350
Today, we record in the
thousands of years every day

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:19.540
of video.

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:21.640
We're running on
hundreds of servers.

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:23.840
And they were still
scaled fairly manually.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.380
They were provisioned
automatically through Puppet

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:29.860
and Cloudyn, and so
forth, but definitely

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:33.030
less mature than we are today.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:36.750
But then the Nest acquisition
was a great opportunity.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:43.650
And it really
supercharged the demands

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:46.680
on our cloud infrastructure.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:51.060
So number one, Nest already
had a foothold in Europe.

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:54.190
And we needed to follow suit.

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:56.220
So very quickly, we had
to move our operations

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:58.510
into a global operation.

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:01.590
The second one, Nest allowed
customers on newer cameras

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:04.530
to turn the sensors
from 720p to 1080p.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.150
And with all the
image recognition

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:07.470
and so forth that we're
doing, that actually

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:09.660
means we need to operate
on about twice the amount

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:11.450
of pixels.

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:16.680
And last but not least,
we have constantly--

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:19.860
over the last four
years, we have constantly

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:23.280
improved our vision
algorithms to actually send

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.290
meaningful events
to our customers.

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:29.340
So that also caused a
significant increase

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:30.810
in just compute demand.

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:35.280
So we are faced with a decision.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:36.400
What are we going to do?

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:39.570
And let's talk through
how we ended up

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:41.610
deciding to go to Google Cloud.

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:44.280
So without a doubt
probably for us,

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:48.220
the biggest reason
was Google global--

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:52.340
the global nature of Google's
network and its components.

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:58.840
So for our service,
all the video

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:00.340
is obviously recorded
in the cloud.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:03.300
But then the video
that is live viewed

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.050
is actually being
reflected off the cloud.

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:07.120
So as such, it's
actually fairly easy

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:09.912
to understand the closer you get
that compute to the customer,

00:07:09.912 --> 00:07:11.620
the better the experience
is going to be,

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:13.420
the lower the latencies
are going to be.

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:17.290
So as we enter new markets,
it's really important for us

00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:20.400
to have a presence
as close as possible.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:25.699
So Google Cloud helps there.

00:07:25.699 --> 00:07:27.240
And we'll look into
that in a second.

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:29.750
So building in
multi-region story

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:33.500
in cloud, whether you do it
for just proximity like us,

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:36.860
or regulatory reasons,
or quote, unquote

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:41.510
"just to achieve resiliency,"
it just seems very easy--

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:43.220
or from experience,
it's very easy

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:44.750
to do it with Google Cloud.

00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:48.290
So that was one.

00:07:48.290 --> 00:07:50.340
The other one was savings.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:53.570
So if you run a business,
you're obviously

00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:55.160
worried about operating costs.

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:58.130
You're worried about margins.

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:02.510
One cost that we are
tracking is the services cost

00:08:02.510 --> 00:08:06.220
of operating a camera.

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:09.280
This is real numbers
without the actual values.

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:13.450
But this is how we have seen
the cost of operating a camera

00:08:13.450 --> 00:08:14.930
decline over time.

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:19.240
Now, I do want to say it's
highly dependent on your use

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:20.180
case.

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:23.170
So in our use case, we actually
stripped everything apart.

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:25.844
Enterprise agreements,
RIs, we just

00:08:25.844 --> 00:08:27.760
stripped that apart,
looked at street pricing.

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:30.070
Compare that to
Google Cloud pricing.

00:08:30.070 --> 00:08:34.630
And with that alone,
we've seen a 20% decrease

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:36.556
in operating cost.

00:08:36.556 --> 00:08:41.400
Now in real numbers, that
means tens of millions

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:42.750
of dollars for us.

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:46.170
And that's just in one
year, very, very massive.

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:49.160
And obviously our finance
team is excited about it.

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:51.590
But we are too because
what it actually allows us

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:53.460
to do it's fund new features.

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:56.060
So we are building
additional features.

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:58.550
And some of the features that
you see on the cameras today

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:01.962
have been funded through those
kind of cost-down efforts

00:09:01.962 --> 00:09:03.170
that we'll continue to drive.

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:08.330
So Google Cloud really
helped us there as well.

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:11.690
So let's take a look at
the migration approach.

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:14.461
So in a nutshell, this
is what it looks like.

00:09:14.461 --> 00:09:16.210
And I really was hoping
this was a smaller

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:17.334
room so I can show up here.

00:09:17.334 --> 00:09:19.160
But I have a laser pointer.

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:21.220
Cameras stream into the cloud.

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:26.440
From there, video
is recorded into S3

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:32.320
and video metadata along with
any event that we have detected

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:35.570
goes into DynamoDB, great.

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:39.390
And then the client, whether you
use your browser or your phone,

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:41.620
rendezvous on the same server.

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:44.560
And you can consume the stream
or watch a recorded video.

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:47.365
Now when we started off, we
sat down with the Google Cloud

00:09:47.365 --> 00:09:49.240
folks and we were like,
hey, couldn't we just

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:52.800
move that S3 thing down
into Google Cloud Storage?

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:53.590
It's a great idea.

00:09:53.590 --> 00:09:55.510
But the way all
public cloud pricing

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:58.940
models work, it's like all
your inbound bandwidth is free.

00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:00.070
But outbound isn't.

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:02.410
So it would have been
really cost prohibitive

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:07.640
to do any hairpin turn in AWS
just to store it into GCP.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:10.570
So what we had to do
instead it's really built up

00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:15.490
the entire stack in
Google Cloud and build

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:20.650
the necessary abstractions for
a server that is in Google Cloud

00:10:20.650 --> 00:10:25.330
to know to talk to Google
Cloud Storage, and to Spanner,

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:28.870
respectively, as a replacement
for S3 and DynamoDB.

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:31.090
So that took a while.

00:10:31.090 --> 00:10:35.180
But it was the way to go.

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:38.390
And then the actual
migration became trivial.

00:10:38.390 --> 00:10:42.520
So we updated the
database record, said

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:44.110
you now belong to Google Cloud.

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:45.950
And we could do this
one camera at a time,

00:10:45.950 --> 00:10:47.850
which is really important.

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:49.470
You now belong to Google Cloud.

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:51.750
We sent the camera
disconnect signal.

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:53.580
The camera dials back in.

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:57.180
It connects to Google Cloud and
starts recording over there.

00:10:57.180 --> 00:10:58.620
Easy, we're done.

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:01.920
This works extremely well,
by the way, for new cameras.

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:04.350
But what about the cameras
that have been recording

00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:07.420
and have data recorded?

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:10.750
Assume they're on a
10-day recording plan.

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:13.720
So what we wanted to
avoid is a big copy job

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:17.440
because ultimately it would
have still been egress charges.

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:21.730
And also if you imagine,
as many of you are users,

00:11:21.730 --> 00:11:25.720
not all the video you have ever
recorded you actually look at.

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:27.250
It's a small
fraction of the video

00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:28.700
that we ever actually play back.

00:11:31.750 --> 00:11:34.250
And on top of that, the data
is actually self-expiring.

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:38.300
So after 10 days, we have a
customer promise that we're

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:39.650
going to delete that data.

00:11:39.650 --> 00:11:42.620
So what we build is a technology
called [INAUDIBLE] gathering.

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:49.380
So if a client
requests video now

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:52.710
connected to Google Cloud
that is still stored in AWS,

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:56.145
we just pull that in
automatically across clouds,

00:11:56.145 --> 00:11:59.660
stitch it back together, and
present it through the API

00:11:59.660 --> 00:12:01.720
as if it would be
all in Google Cloud.

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:06.290
Now, it's very specific
to our use case,

00:12:06.290 --> 00:12:11.420
but what's interesting here is--

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:21.130
what's interesting
here is you can

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:24.070
do this with any application.

00:12:24.070 --> 00:12:29.110
Even if you have
S3 log processing

00:12:29.110 --> 00:12:31.060
and the data is
expiring over there,

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:35.560
you can easily use public
cloud APIs securely

00:12:35.560 --> 00:12:36.520
over the internet.

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:38.710
Bringing back the
LEGO analogy, there's

00:12:38.710 --> 00:12:41.680
no reason why you
can't intermingle

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:42.820
those different services.

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:45.490
So it was a breakthrough
moment for us

00:12:45.490 --> 00:12:48.130
to really keep migration
costs under control.

00:12:52.930 --> 00:12:55.150
Next, I want to
talk about the how

00:12:55.150 --> 00:12:56.920
we build a global architecture.

00:12:56.920 --> 00:13:01.171
So to do that, I want to
talk through real quick how

00:13:01.171 --> 00:13:02.170
a camera first connects.

00:13:02.170 --> 00:13:04.270
When you first
power it up, it goes

00:13:04.270 --> 00:13:08.020
through this routing
lookup layer where,

00:13:08.020 --> 00:13:10.990
backed by load balancer, that--

00:13:13.194 --> 00:13:15.360
backed by a load balancer,
which, in turn, is backed

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:17.940
by Compute Engine instances.

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:20.694
And we do a quick
lookup figuring out

00:13:20.694 --> 00:13:22.110
where this camera
belongs and then

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:25.040
actually connect this camera
straight to the owning server

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:26.320
of that camera.

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:31.840
Now very quickly, you can
add a couple of regions.

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:34.470
And now really try to
look at both ends of this,

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:36.860
whether it's the load
balancer on the left side,

00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:39.170
with a couple of
API calls or clicks,

00:13:39.170 --> 00:13:42.530
you can add compute
engines from any region

00:13:42.530 --> 00:13:43.912
really to that load balancer.

00:13:43.912 --> 00:13:45.620
And if you look at it
from the right side

00:13:45.620 --> 00:13:49.250
as well, whether it's
the global Spanner

00:13:49.250 --> 00:13:58.550
storage or within a geographic
region, a Google Cloud Storage

00:13:58.550 --> 00:14:01.790
bucket, all these things, and
other services that are not

00:14:01.790 --> 00:14:04.700
showing, such as Pub/Sub,
just are global in nature,

00:14:04.700 --> 00:14:07.549
making it very easy to build
a global infrastructure.

00:14:12.820 --> 00:14:16.020
The other thing that's important
here, and it's not shown,

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:18.510
is it's really the
same VPC network,

00:14:18.510 --> 00:14:21.270
it's the same firewalls,
it's the same public APIs

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:24.120
that we've called, and
the same public API

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:27.060
endpoints to launch
these instances.

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:29.070
It's really just a
matter of specifying

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:32.520
the launch region of where
you launch your instance.

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:34.080
And last but not
least, all of them

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:36.010
are connected through
Google's fast backbone.

00:14:36.010 --> 00:14:39.236
So even if you have
cross-region traffic,

00:14:39.236 --> 00:14:41.610
you're don't to have to deal
with VPC peering or anything

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:42.110
like it.

00:14:42.110 --> 00:14:44.850
It just works.

00:14:44.850 --> 00:14:48.460
Next, we can just add
an entire continent.

00:14:48.460 --> 00:14:54.290
So going into Europe, for
example, going into Asia next,

00:14:54.290 --> 00:14:56.000
now it's very
cookie cutter to go

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:58.280
into new markets
and new regions.

00:14:58.280 --> 00:15:05.180
And SRE or operations
is now no longer

00:15:05.180 --> 00:15:07.940
in the way of the business
to enter new markets, which

00:15:07.940 --> 00:15:09.080
is really a great enabler.

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:20.200
Yeah, that's how we build
a global architecture.

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:24.760
There's three takeaways I think
that I want to talk about.

00:15:24.760 --> 00:15:27.370
And the first one is
focus on reusable tools.

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:31.810
So doing a migration like
this is a huge investment.

00:15:31.810 --> 00:15:34.810
So in our case, we've written
thousands of lines of codes.

00:15:34.810 --> 00:15:39.250
And at every step and every
turn, we've asked ourselves,

00:15:39.250 --> 00:15:41.260
is this a one-time deal,
or is this something

00:15:41.260 --> 00:15:43.840
we could actually
reuse in the future?

00:15:43.840 --> 00:15:47.770
So remember [INAUDIBLE]
gathering, how we brought

00:15:47.770 --> 00:15:50.530
things from AWS on-demand over?

00:15:50.530 --> 00:15:53.800
So imagine whether
it's a future feature

00:15:53.800 --> 00:15:57.860
or something we do automatic
as part of like a DR strategy

00:15:57.860 --> 00:15:58.360
or so.

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:04.220
But let's imagine we're moving
a camera from the US to Europe.

00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:06.610
Why wouldn't we want to
use [INAUDIBLE] gathering

00:16:06.610 --> 00:16:10.030
that we've now once built
to pull in data on demand

00:16:10.030 --> 00:16:12.940
from other regions if we have
to and then stitch it together

00:16:12.940 --> 00:16:13.780
and present it back?

00:16:13.780 --> 00:16:16.180
Imagine a camera
or an owner moving

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:17.322
taking the camera with it.

00:16:17.322 --> 00:16:19.030
You can take it as
far as, in the future,

00:16:19.030 --> 00:16:23.480
sensing of where that
camera is best connected.

00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:26.419
So again, it's just one
example of re-usability.

00:16:26.419 --> 00:16:28.210
It's something that we
really, really focus

00:16:28.210 --> 00:16:32.300
on during this migration.

00:16:32.300 --> 00:16:35.930
My next favorite topic is to
think outside of the Ops box.

00:16:39.390 --> 00:16:42.780
This is probably the
most important thing

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:45.100
that I learned during
this migration.

00:16:45.100 --> 00:16:47.090
So I run SRE.

00:16:47.090 --> 00:16:51.720
And I was tasked with
getting the service over

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:54.030
to Google Cloud.

00:16:54.030 --> 00:16:59.860
But my lesson here is whether
you're SRE, PE, DevOps,

00:16:59.860 --> 00:17:03.360
NetOps, make it known
within your organization

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:08.470
that this is really a
cross-functional exercise.

00:17:08.470 --> 00:17:12.730
And if you do it well, and you
really focus on the customer,

00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:17.810
you can really build
a seamless experience

00:17:17.810 --> 00:17:20.220
or make the migration
extremely seamless.

00:17:20.220 --> 00:17:25.890
And you actually end up or
possibly-- and I'll show that--

00:17:25.890 --> 00:17:28.300
in a better place
than you were before.

00:17:28.300 --> 00:17:30.730
So I want to show
two quick examples.

00:17:30.730 --> 00:17:33.600
Number one is for the
live viewing case.

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:38.010
So if you do this poorly,
you can easily get to a point

00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:39.810
where, OK, we
migrate the camera.

00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:42.420
First of all, we try to
avoid migrating a camera

00:17:42.420 --> 00:17:43.989
while it's actively
being viewed.

00:17:43.989 --> 00:17:46.530
But let's assume for a second
you migrate a camera while it's

00:17:46.530 --> 00:17:47.645
actively being viewed.

00:17:47.645 --> 00:17:49.520
You move it over, send
the disconnect signal,

00:17:49.520 --> 00:17:52.160
it comes back in.

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:56.430
If a customer is viewing
this camera feed,

00:17:56.430 --> 00:17:58.769
it turns black, customer
has to exit the app,

00:17:58.769 --> 00:17:59.810
go back in, and so forth.

00:17:59.810 --> 00:18:00.900
It's frustrating.

00:18:00.900 --> 00:18:03.180
So we work very closely
with the apps developers

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:05.460
to say, hey, if we
send you a signal

00:18:05.460 --> 00:18:08.880
that we just changed regions,
can you just reestablish

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:10.292
a spinner for a second?

00:18:10.292 --> 00:18:11.750
And that's effectively
what we did.

00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:14.790
But it actually now
improves the resiliency

00:18:14.790 --> 00:18:17.910
and our communications signaling
between the apps and cloud.

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:19.200
So it's one example.

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:23.190
The other one is around
the recording model.

00:18:23.190 --> 00:18:25.977
So the cameras-- and this
is something where we worked

00:18:25.977 --> 00:18:27.060
with the [INAUDIBLE] team.

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:29.230
Again, we own end-to-end
of the communication.

00:18:29.230 --> 00:18:30.870
So we worked with
the [INAUDIBLE] team.

00:18:30.870 --> 00:18:33.540
And we used something
we call backfill.

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:36.210
So the camera in itself has
a little two-minute buffer.

00:18:36.210 --> 00:18:38.400
So when we send the
disconnect signal

00:18:38.400 --> 00:18:42.150
to come back into Google
Cloud, it's five seconds.

00:18:42.150 --> 00:18:43.890
On the other side
in Google Cloud,

00:18:43.890 --> 00:18:45.770
the camera will negotiate
with the service

00:18:45.770 --> 00:18:47.460
to say, hey, where
did you left off

00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:51.280
recording over in AWS or
anywhere else for that matter?

00:18:51.280 --> 00:18:55.950
And then we are negotiating
and stitching the timeline

00:18:55.950 --> 00:18:56.880
back together.

00:18:56.880 --> 00:19:00.030
So as a result, if you come back
later that day and your camera

00:19:00.030 --> 00:19:01.950
had been migrated,
you absolutely

00:19:01.950 --> 00:19:06.040
see no seams on your timeline,
which is pretty neat.

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:08.460
So again, it took a lot
of effort to get there.

00:19:08.460 --> 00:19:13.200
But it's like, we were that
concerned about the customer

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:16.870
experience during
this migration.

00:19:16.870 --> 00:19:20.580
And then the other thing
is re-architecting.

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:23.280
And do it often.

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:25.060
If I would have
waited, or we would

00:19:25.060 --> 00:19:29.170
have waited for Google
Cloud to provide us

00:19:29.170 --> 00:19:32.380
with a replica of
what we had in Amazon,

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:34.720
we would have never moved.

00:19:34.720 --> 00:19:38.050
So you do need to adapt.

00:19:38.050 --> 00:19:40.780
And if you look at the
marketing material,

00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:42.700
very often what I
found is it scratches

00:19:42.700 --> 00:19:45.600
the surface of what the actual
performance characteristics are

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:46.840
of their services.

00:19:46.840 --> 00:19:49.360
And even if you look
things like S3 and GCS,

00:19:49.360 --> 00:19:52.420
they're ever so
subtly different.

00:19:52.420 --> 00:19:56.890
So as you go down
this road, I encourage

00:19:56.890 --> 00:19:59.770
you to stay very nimble
and really embrace that,

00:19:59.770 --> 00:20:01.330
that you need to
make changes a lot.

00:20:01.330 --> 00:20:03.760
So here's a very
simple example that you

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:04.850
can wrap your head around.

00:20:04.850 --> 00:20:11.050
So the average recording node in
AWS looked something like that.

00:20:13.870 --> 00:20:17.440
So we are using
c3 instance types.

00:20:17.440 --> 00:20:18.940
And we really like
them because they

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:21.730
come for free with these
awesome ephemeral drives,

00:20:21.730 --> 00:20:26.320
super high IOPS, two times 80
gig, we strap them together.

00:20:26.320 --> 00:20:29.800
And we use them
both to spool video

00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:31.660
before we flush it out to S3.

00:20:31.660 --> 00:20:36.084
But we also use it to keep
a cache of recent video

00:20:36.084 --> 00:20:37.750
so we don't actually
have to retrieve it

00:20:37.750 --> 00:20:40.720
from S3 every time a
customer requests something.

00:20:40.720 --> 00:20:44.710
Now we're like, OK, so we
can build the same thing over

00:20:44.710 --> 00:20:45.460
in Compute Engine.

00:20:45.460 --> 00:20:48.400
So we were like, oh, let's
take an n1-standard-8.

00:20:48.400 --> 00:20:51.100
Comes to a 30 gig of RAM,
really very similar performance

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:52.790
characteristics.

00:20:52.790 --> 00:20:58.400
And we plugged a hard
drive in, a PD 160 gigs.

00:20:58.400 --> 00:21:00.690
And we're like, oh, this
is going to be great.

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:02.750
It worked phenomenally
while in QA.

00:21:02.750 --> 00:21:07.760
But once we actually put it
in-- like load under it--

00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:11.510
load tested it, turns out--
and it's not the fault of PD--

00:21:11.510 --> 00:21:15.890
we just like, it doesn't
match the I/O capacity

00:21:15.890 --> 00:21:18.170
of a ephemeral SSD drive.

00:21:18.170 --> 00:21:21.090
So we're like, OK,
that doesn't work.

00:21:21.090 --> 00:21:23.780
So next, Google has a similar--

00:21:23.780 --> 00:21:26.330
and this is why I'm saying both
of them check the checkmarks.

00:21:26.330 --> 00:21:28.310
They have ephemeral drives.

00:21:28.310 --> 00:21:33.770
Google has a drive that's
375 gigs local SSD.

00:21:33.770 --> 00:21:34.650
It's S volatile.

00:21:34.650 --> 00:21:36.330
It's S fast.

00:21:36.330 --> 00:21:39.140
The problem is
though, it's too big.

00:21:39.140 --> 00:21:42.200
It's the smallest
denominator that you

00:21:42.200 --> 00:21:43.865
can attach in local SSD.

00:21:47.127 --> 00:21:48.710
For most customers,
it's not a problem

00:21:48.710 --> 00:21:50.960
because they actually
attach 10 or so of them

00:21:50.960 --> 00:21:52.190
to run big Cassandra nodes.

00:21:52.190 --> 00:21:54.930
But in our case, if you operate
thousands of these servers,

00:21:54.930 --> 00:21:56.820
it would have just
been a giant waste.

00:21:56.820 --> 00:21:58.530
So we ruled that out as well.

00:21:58.530 --> 00:22:02.600
And then we started
thinking, let's

00:22:02.600 --> 00:22:06.230
take a look at the 160 gigs and
see what we actually used them

00:22:06.230 --> 00:22:07.160
for.

00:22:07.160 --> 00:22:14.290
So we added instrumentation
around your cache hit rates,

00:22:14.290 --> 00:22:16.500
or cache miss
rates, and so forth.

00:22:16.500 --> 00:22:19.340
And it turns out we actually
didn't need all that storage.

00:22:19.340 --> 00:22:22.220
So we ended up with
a machine-- and this

00:22:22.220 --> 00:22:25.840
was all in a matter of
a week or so, so again,

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:27.620
staying very, very nimble.

00:22:27.620 --> 00:22:32.140
We ended up with a machine
that was an n1-himem-8.

00:22:32.140 --> 00:22:33.130
That's how it's called.

00:22:33.130 --> 00:22:34.546
So it just comes
with more memory.

00:22:34.546 --> 00:22:36.550
And we carved up
to 25 megabytes--

00:22:36.550 --> 00:22:39.790
sorry, 25 gigabyte hard drive.

00:22:39.790 --> 00:22:43.810
And it's S volatile
as a local SSD.

00:22:43.810 --> 00:22:47.770
It has even better
IOPS performance.

00:22:47.770 --> 00:22:50.064
And in the process, we
saved a bunch of money

00:22:50.064 --> 00:22:51.730
as well because we
didn't have to attach

00:22:51.730 --> 00:22:53.050
all these additional drives.

00:22:53.050 --> 00:22:56.570
So it's that kind of thing where
once the rubber hits the road,

00:22:56.570 --> 00:22:59.200
you really need to make
adjustments, and embrace

00:22:59.200 --> 00:23:00.670
that, and be open to that.

00:23:00.670 --> 00:23:02.398
So great.

00:23:05.870 --> 00:23:09.062
So takeaways.

00:23:12.300 --> 00:23:20.120
This ties back into
the hard drive story.

00:23:20.120 --> 00:23:21.760
Don't be afraid to experiment.

00:23:21.760 --> 00:23:25.840
My number one advice is invest
in good instrumentation.

00:23:25.840 --> 00:23:27.400
You want to set the
baseline of what

00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:31.540
the customer expects is
experiencing in your old cloud

00:23:31.540 --> 00:23:33.500
provider.

00:23:33.500 --> 00:23:36.250
And then you really want to say
with confidence whether you're

00:23:36.250 --> 00:23:37.916
moving the needle in
the right direction

00:23:37.916 --> 00:23:40.950
or in the wrong direction.

00:23:40.950 --> 00:23:43.040
The other thing I feel
fairly strongly about

00:23:43.040 --> 00:23:44.679
is forklifts don't work.

00:23:44.679 --> 00:23:46.220
You don't want to
come in on a Monday

00:23:46.220 --> 00:23:49.200
and change DNS of
your entire service.

00:23:49.200 --> 00:23:57.360
I really try to move either
one customer at a time.

00:23:57.360 --> 00:23:59.720
In our case, it didn't work
due to data constraints.

00:23:59.720 --> 00:24:02.270
But if you can, one
service at a time,

00:24:02.270 --> 00:24:04.100
have a fallback
strategy, and so forth.

00:24:08.150 --> 00:24:10.760
We did that more
than once where we--

00:24:10.760 --> 00:24:13.230
in the early phases where
we moved a couple of cameras

00:24:13.230 --> 00:24:16.240
over and then we moved them
back, internal cameras.

00:24:19.650 --> 00:24:25.080
And then as I said before, it's
like hitting six right numbers

00:24:25.080 --> 00:24:27.066
in the lottery, you're
not going to pick

00:24:27.066 --> 00:24:28.690
the right infrastructure
on the get go.

00:24:28.690 --> 00:24:32.220
So really, I encourage you
to stay nimble and actually

00:24:32.220 --> 00:24:35.430
embrace that fact to make
changes often and see

00:24:35.430 --> 00:24:39.090
if you move the needle
in one way or the other.

00:24:39.090 --> 00:24:40.950
That's one.

00:24:40.950 --> 00:24:43.330
The other one is
focus on reusability.

00:24:43.330 --> 00:24:47.910
So I said before, it's not
SRE, PE, DevOps problem alone.

00:24:47.910 --> 00:24:50.130
Really try to focus
on the customer.

00:24:50.130 --> 00:24:53.940
And you do have an opportunity,
just as I showed you to,

00:24:53.940 --> 00:24:57.570
actually come out with a
service and a platform that

00:24:57.570 --> 00:25:00.380
is better and more stable than
it was before you actually went

00:25:00.380 --> 00:25:07.000
down on this journey to migrate
to cloud or to Google Cloud.

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:14.157
And last but not least, save
money and have fun with it.

00:25:14.157 --> 00:25:15.240
It's something that we do.

00:25:15.240 --> 00:25:18.080
It's in our DNA to constantly
create additional headroom

00:25:18.080 --> 00:25:18.830
for features.

00:25:18.830 --> 00:25:21.560
And we've just scratched
the surface on it.

00:25:21.560 --> 00:25:24.590
I mean, sustained use
discounts, you get for free.

00:25:24.590 --> 00:25:30.274
But then Google has great
knobs to really drive

00:25:30.274 --> 00:25:31.440
your operational costs down.

00:25:31.440 --> 00:25:34.190
So we are looking very
heavily into autoscaling.

00:25:34.190 --> 00:25:37.400
And we're very excited
about preemptible VMs

00:25:37.400 --> 00:25:39.920
in particular for
some of our workloads

00:25:39.920 --> 00:25:41.540
to reduce costs even further.

00:25:41.540 --> 00:25:44.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

